WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:03.300
Let's go next to our caller from the five
41 area code.

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.000
Who's calling today from five four one.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.700
Hi David.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:09.240
This is Jordan from Eugene, Oregon.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.240
Hey Jordan.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:11.240
What's up?

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:12.240
Hi.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:16.810
I'm, I tweeted you about this the other day,
but I thought I'd call you for the first time.

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:23.330
Um, and I had a thought, um, when it comes
to the impeachment, and right now Nancy Polosi

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:27.130
is holding, uh, the articles are going to
the Senate if we're going to trial.

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:29.029
So it's kinda this weird stalemate.

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:35.160
Is it possible that we can go into the next
presidential election assuming that Trump

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:42.100
loses office and someone else gets in, that
they can actually have a trial after there

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.260
is a new president on Trump?

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:47.850
You know, I don't know the answer to that
to be perfectly Frank.

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:51.690
So, so the idea would be Nancy Pelosi holds
articles of impeachment.

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:56.449
The president has been impeached, uh, then
Trump loses and then you have a different

00:00:56.449 --> 00:00:59.590
president in, but you still do an impeachment
trial of Trump.

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:05.440
I think that you prop, my instinct is you
can't, because impeachment is an impeachment

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:09.190
trial is ultimately a vote about whether to
remove the president.

00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:12.990
If the president's already gone, it makes
it moot.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:16.200
And maybe it could still have some symbolic
meaning, but I don't think so.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.470
I think you've got to do, I think you've got
to do the impeachment trial while the president

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:22.060
is still president.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:25.940
That's, I mean, that makes a lot of sense.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:30.320
The only thing I was thinking about was like,
cause technically since no one has ever been

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:36.970
convicted in my mind, uh, from a trial, uh,
that so there's never really been a consequence

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:43.500
other than removing a president cause there
potentially could be additional consequences

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.240
rather than just other than just being removed.

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:47.670
That's why I was thinking that way.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:51.930
Well there can be, once the president is no
longer president, they can conceivably could

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:53.320
be indicted for crimes.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:02:00.200
But, um, it's, it's, uh, I, I think our priority
should be let's remove him in November no

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:01.280
matter what.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:06.660
And at a certain point, let's, let's do the
trial in the Senate probably before the election.

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:10.590
I think it has to be, but maybe Nancy Pelosi
knows better than I do.

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:12.730
Yeah, it's a good possibility.

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:15.380
But, um, I enjoyed hearing your thoughts,
David.

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:16.380
Thank you very much.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:17.380
All right, thank you.

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:18.380
Appreciate the call.

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:18.383
Thanks very much.

